    {
      "t": "rule",
      "title": "4 · Con BE y LOOK → adjetivo",
      "title_en": "be / look / feel + adjective",
      "icon": "🪞",
      "color": "pink",
      "es": "¡Cuidado! Después de <b>be</b> (am, is, are) y de verbos como <b>look</b> (parecer), <b>feel</b> (sentirse), <b>sound</b> (sonar) o <b>smell</b> (oler), usamos el <b>adjetivo</b>, no el adverbio. Estos verbos no son acciones: dicen cómo <b>es</b> o cómo <b>parece</b> algo.",
      "en": "After <b>be, look, feel, sound, smell</b> we use an <b>adjective</b>, not an adverb.",
      "formula": [
        "be / look / feel + *adjetivo*"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "You look <b>happy</b> today!",
          "es": "¡Hoy pareces contento!",
          "icon": "😊"
        },
        {
          "en": "This pizza smells <b>good</b>.",
          "es": "Esta pizza huele bien.",
          "icon": "🍕"
        },
        {
          "en": "I feel <b>tired</b> after football.",
          "es": "Me siento cansado después del fútbol.",
          "icon": "😴"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "rule",
      "title": "5 · VERY, REALLY y HOW?",
      "title_en": "very, really and How?",
      "icon": "❓",
      "color": "purple",
      "es": "Para hacer el adverbio más fuerte, ponemos <b>very</b> o <b>really</b> delante: very <b>quickly</b>, really <b>well</b>. Y para preguntar por el modo usamos <b>How…?</b>: <i>How does Leo run?</i>",
      "en": "Use <b>very</b> or <b>really</b> before the adverb. Ask about manner with <b>How…?</b>",
      "formula": [
        "*How* + does he play?",
        "He plays + *really well*"
      ],
      "examples": [
        {
          "en": "How does Rocky run? — <b>Very fast!</b>",
